(§ II. PLACE OF ECONOMICS AMONG THE SOCIAL SCIENCES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Economics contrasted with the natural sciences]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _Political economy, as one of the social sciences, may be contrasted) Tj T*
(with the natural sciences, which deal with material things and their) Tj T*
(mutual relations, while it deals with one aspect of men's life in) Tj T*
(society, namely, the earning of a living, or the use of wealth._ It is) Tj T*
(true that political economy also has to do with plants and animals and) Tj T*
(the earth--in fact, with all of those things which are the) Tj T*
(subject-matter of the natural sciences; but it has to do with them only) Tj T*
(in so far as they are related to man's welfare and affect his estimate) Tj T*
(of the value of things; only in so far as they are related to the one) Tj T*
(central subject of economic interest, the earning of a living.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Character of the social sciences]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _The social sciences deal with men and their relations with each) Tj T*
(other._ The word "social" comes from the Latin socius, meaning a fellow,) Tj T*
(comrade, companion, associate. As men living together have to do with) Tj T*
(each other in a great many different ways, and enter into a great many) Tj T*
(different relations, there arise a great many different social problems.) Tj T*
(Each of the social sciences attempts to study man in some one important) Tj T*
(aspect--that is, to view these relations from some one standpoint.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Man's acts, his life, and his motives are so complex that it is not) Tj T*
(surprising that there has been less definiteness in the thought of the) Tj T*
(social sciences, and that they have advanced less rapidly toward) Tj T*
(exactness in their conclusions, than have the natural sciences. This) Tj T*
(complexity also explains the discouragement of the beginner in the early) Tj T*
(lessons in this subject. Usually the greatest difficulties appear in the) Tj T*
(first few weeks of its study. The thought is more abstract than in) Tj T*
(natural science; it requires a different, I will not say higher, kind of) Tj T*
(ability than does mathematics. But little by little the strangeness of) Tj T*
(the language and ideas disappears; the bare definitions become clothed) Tj T*
(with the facts of observation and recalled experiences; and soon the) Tj T*
("economic" acts and relations of men in society come to be as real and) Tj T*
(as interesting to the student as are the materials in the natural world) Tj T*
(about him--often, indeed, more interesting.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Economics, politics, law, and ethics]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
