(§ III. THE RELATION OF ECONOMICS TO PRACTICAL AFFAIRS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Economics is first a science]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _The ideal of political economy here set forth is that it should be a) Tj T*
(science, a search for truth, a systematized body of knowledge, arriving) Tj T*
(at a statement of the laws to which economic actions conform._ It is not) Tj T*
(the advocacy of any particular policy or idea, but if it arrives at any) Tj T*
(conclusions, any truths, these cannot fail to affect the practical) Tj T*
(action of men.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: But it touches many practical interests]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Political economy, because defined as the science of wealth, has been) Tj T*
(described by some as a gospel of Mammon. It is hardly necessary to) Tj T*
(refute such a misconception. Political economy is not the science of) Tj T*
(wealth-getting for the individual. Its study is not primarily for the) Tj T*
(selfish ends and interest of the individual. \(Certainly some of its) Tj T*
(lessons may be of practical value to men in active business\) for many) Tj T*
(economic "principles" are but the general statement of those ideas that) Tj T*
(have been approved by the experience of business men, of statesmen, and) Tj T*
(of the masses of men. Some of its lessons must have educational value in) Tj T*
(practical business, for political economy is not dreamed out by the) Tj T*
(closet philosopher, but more and more it is the attempt to describe the) Tj T*
(interests and the action of the practical world in which men must live.) Tj T*
(Many men are working together to develop its study--those who collect) Tj T*
(statistics and facts bearing on all kinds of practical affairs, and) Tj T*
(those who search through the records of the past for illustrations of) Tj T*
(experiments and experiences that may help us in our life to-day.) Tj T*
