(§ I. MATERIAL WANTS, THE PRIMARY ECONOMIC MOTIVES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Feeling urges to economic actions]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _A logical explanation of industry must begin with a discussion of) Tj T*
(the nature of wants, for the purpose of industry is to gratify wants._) Tj T*
(An economic want may be defined as a feeling of incompleteness, because) Tj T*
(of the lack of a part of the outer world or of some change in it. Often) Tj T*
(the question asked when one first sees a moving trolley car or) Tj T*
(automobile or bicycle is: What makes it go? The first question to ask in) Tj T*
(the part of the study of economic society here undertaken is: What is) Tj T*
(its motive force? Without an answer to that question one cannot hope to) Tj T*
(understand the ceaseless and varied activities of men occupied in the) Tj T*
(making of a living. The question merits long and careful study, but the) Tj T*
(general answer is so simple that it seems almost self-evident: The) Tj T*
(motive force in economics is found in the feelings of men. It is men's) Tj T*
(desire to make use of men and things about them which calls forth all) Tj T*
(the manifold phenomena studied in economics.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Animal species shaped by their environment]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _Wants among animals depend on the environment; that is to say, the) Tj T*
(utmost that creatures of a lower order than man can do is to take things) Tj T*
(as they find them._ The imagination and intelligence of animals are not) Tj T*
(developed enough to lead them to desire much beyond that which is) Tj T*
(ordinarily to be obtained. And so the environment shapes and affects the) Tj T*
(animal. The fish is fitted to live in the water and thrives there, and) Tj T*
(we must believe, enjoys living there. The horse and the cow like best) Tj T*
(the food of the fields, and so each species of animal, in order to) Tj T*
(survive in the severe struggle for existence, has been forced to fit) Tj T*
(itself to the conditions in which it lives. After the animal has been) Tj T*
(thus fitted, its desire is for those things normally to be found in its) Tj T*
(surroundings. So different animals desire or want different things, but) Tj T*
(always it is the environment that determines the want, and not the want) Tj T*
(that determines the environment.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Simple wants of primitive men]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. _In simpler human societies, wants are mostly confined to physical) Tj T*
(necessities; that is, in the earlier stages of society, man's wants are) Tj T*
(very much like those of the animals._ Man bends his energies to securing) Tj T*
(the things necessary to survival. He feels the pangs of hunger and he) Tj T*
(strives to secure food. He feels the need of companionship, for it is) Tj T*
(only through association and mutual help that men, so weak as compared) Tj T*
(with many kinds of animals, are able to resist the enemies which beset) Tj T*
(them. He needs clothing to protect him against the harsher climates of) Tj T*
(the lands to which he moves. For the same purpose, to protect himself) Tj T*
(against the cold and rain, he needs a shelter, a cave, a wigwam, or a) Tj T*
(hut; for a house is but a larger dress.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Manifold wants in civilized society]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
ET
