(1. _Satisfaction and gratification being only temporary conditions,) Tj T*
(economic wants appear in more or less regularly recurring series._) Tj T*
(Impressions are short lived, sensations are temporary, wants that have) Tj T*
(been satisfied recur. Wants recur for the same reason that they first) Tj T*
(arose. No impression on the nerves or on the senses is lasting. Man's) Tj T*
(senses were developed for the purpose of bringing him into relation with) Tj T*
(the outer world, of enabling him to survive in his struggle with the) Tj T*
(forces of nature. So, when a good has been enjoyed, the utility to that) Tj T*
(person of that thing or service for that particular moment, falls, it) Tj T*
(may be even to zero. To keep wants satisfied is impossible; we cannot do) Tj T*
(next year's reading or next week's eating now; we cannot live the life) Tj T*
(of to-morrow. The best results in reading or eating come from taking the) Tj T*
(right amount day by day. But it is a need in the life of men that wants) Tj T*
(should recur after a time, otherwise there would be no motive for) Tj T*
(action.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Series of wants and series of goods]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _The economic ideal is that this series of recurring wants should be) Tj T*
(met by a corresponding series of goods._ It is evident that if a series) Tj T*
(or succession of goods varies, at different times, moments, and) Tj T*
(conditions, in its power to gratify wants, the closer the correspondence) Tj T*
(between the two series, that of wants and that of goods, the greater) Tj T*
(will be the total of gratification. We may liken man's life to a journey) Tj T*
(in which the supplies of food are gotten at the stations. If any one of) Tj T*
(these supplies fails, the traveler suffers the pangs of hunger, and if) Tj T*
(two or three supplies are at one point, they do not serve the needs of) Tj T*
(man so well as if distributed along the way. This constant inflow of) Tj T*
(goods is one of the fundamental needs of life. The savage dimly) Tj T*
(understands this need. Even the birds and the beasts adjust their lives) Tj T*
(to it either by travel or by toil. The spring and autumn migrations to) Tj T*
(new feeding grounds are the attempts of the bird to gratify this series) Tj T*
(of wants as they arise. The ant, the bee, and the squirrel anticipate,) Tj T*
(and work to fill their storehouses against the days of need.) Tj T*
