(value of a temporary use for a brief period, as for a year; and that of) Tj T*
(the value of a thing itself, the use-bearer, for a long series of years) Tj T*
(or in perpetuity. To explain what fixes the value of the temporary use) Tj T*
(is the problem of rent; to explain what determines the value of) Tj T*
(long-continued use or of permanent control and ownership of a use-bearer) Tj T*
(is the problem of capitalization.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Origin of the term rent]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _The term rent is used in a number of senses, which must be carefully) Tj T*
(distinguished._ The original meaning of rent was any regular income or) Tj T*
(revenue arising from wealth. The word comes from the low Latin _renta_) Tj T*
(from _renda_, in turn from _redditus_, that which is given, yielded or) Tj T*
(given back, or _rendita_, that which is given or returned. The French) Tj T*
(_rendre_ \(English render\), to give or return that which belongs to one,) Tj T*
(is used very early. Chaucer used "rente" as an income. "Cattle had he) Tj T*
(enough and rente," cattle probably meaning property \(chattels\), and) Tj T*
(rente income. Rental is a collective term for a number of rents. The) Tj T*
(total yield of an estate was called its rental or rent-roll, and a list) Tj T*
(of the various sources of income, including all payments from tenants in) Tj T*
(money, produce or services, constituted its rental.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Popular and special meaning of rent]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. _The popular meaning of rent is the amount paid for the use of) Tj T*
(material things which must be returned to the owners after the time of) Tj T*
(use agreed upon._ We speak of the rent of a house, boat, etc., using the) Tj T*
(word as a synonym for hire. In the European languages the word is used) Tj T*
(more frequently in that sense. In the French _la rente_ means the income) Tj T*
(from any kind of property; but corporate securities and national bonds) Tj T*
