(§ II. EFFECTS OF SOCIAL CHANGES IN RAISING THE RENTS OF INDIRECT AGENTS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Effect of decrease of the competing agents]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _Changes in the number and kind of competing resources may raise the) Tj T*
(rents of particular agents._ Rents may increase without increase in the) Tj T*
(quantity or number of a particular group of agents or without change in) Tj T*
(their technical efficiency. As changes in the conditions of society may) Tj T*
(reduce rents, so other changes may increase them. Agents of the same) Tj T*
(kind may diminish in number, either absolutely or relatively. If some of) Tj T*
(the competing machines are destroyed, the rents of the machines that) Tj T*
(remain rise, while if new supplies are found, either in nature or by) Tj T*
(improved industrial processes, the rents of the older agents fall.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
