(utility of goods, and that such differences have influence on exchange.) Tj T*
(Now in exchange by barter such a small estimate is impossible. In barter) Tj T*
(things are exchanged directly for each other in kind. If the two things) Tj T*
(do not chance to coincide in value, the exchange cannot be completed. An) Tj T*
(equivalent must be found, or a multiple, if the marginal utility of two) Tj T*
(goods is to be equalized for either party by exchange. As in most cases) Tj T*
(this adjustment must be very incomplete, many exchanges that otherwise) Tj T*
(would be advantageous cannot take place. In the earlier stages of) Tj T*
(development, this careful estimate of value is not found. Children do) Tj T*
(not make it. The typical trade of the small boy is a "trade even";) Tj T*
(Johnny exchanges his gingerbread for Jimmie's jack-knife. It marks an) Tj T*
(epoch in the industrial development of the boy when he begins to keep) Tj T*
(store with pins, and no longer trades candy for apples, but both for) Tj T*
(pins, which have become the medium of exchange in his boy world. He then) Tj T*
(can express values in much more exact terms. In our society most) Tj T*
(children begin early to grow familiar with this conception; but) Tj T*
(travelers find some savage tribes still in the earlier childish stage of) Tj T*
(development, unable to grasp the thought of a general medium of) Tj T*
(exchange. When, through lack of a medium of exchange, there is a failure) Tj T*
(to adjust utilities, there is a loss of the possible advantage in each) Tj T*
(defeated exchange. There is a further waste of time and of vain efforts) Tj T*
(to find something that will be accepted in exchange, and the loss) Tj T*
(offsets a large part of the gain even when the barter is effected.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
