([Sidenote: Money is an indirect agent, a tool to effect exchanges]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _Money in all its money uses is an indirect agent, to be judged just) Tj T*
(as other indirect agents are._ The key to this section is the thought) Tj T*
(that the function of money is to serve as an indirect agent. Money is) Tj T*
(often, by a figure of speech, called a tool. Literally a tool is a bit) Tj T*
(of material which, taken in the hand, is used to apply force to other) Tj T*
(things, to shape them or move them. Figuratively, this is just what) Tj T*
(money does. A man takes it in his hand not to get enjoyment out of it,) Tj T*
(but to apply force, to move something, and that which he moves is the) Tj T*
(other commodity. Adam Smith aptly likened money to the road and wagons) Tj T*
(that transport goods, thus gratifying wants by putting things into a) Tj T*
(more convenient place. Money is only one of a multitude of forms of) Tj T*
(wealth. It is not even the most "valuable"; it has value just as other) Tj T*
(indirect agents have. The loss caused by taking away an indirect agent) Tj T*
(entirely is greater than the benefit usually attributed to it. Its) Tj T*
(utility in the extremest conditions is greater than its marginal utility) Tj T*
(under ordinary conditions. Food is not credited in the market with) Tj T*
(enormous value, but if starvation threatened, all else would be given) Tj T*
(for food. In a like manner, each individual values money according to) Tj T*
(the importance of the marginal service it renders, but the marginal) Tj T*
(service is far from measuring the loss that would be caused by the) Tj T*
(entire disuse of money. In a society without money, industrial processes) Tj T*
(would be very different, and exchange would be hampered in almost) Tj T*
(inconceivable ways. It is true, therefore, that money is an economic) Tj T*
(factor of high importance, but it is not so indispensable as many other) Tj T*
(factors to which far less value is attributed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Why a poor community lacks money]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A poor community has little money because it cannot afford more; it) Tj T*
(gets along with less money than is convenient just as it gets along with) Tj T*
(fewer indirect agents of every other kind than it could use. Pioneers in) Tj T*
(a poor community where the average wealth is low, cannot afford to keep) Tj T*
(a large number of wagons, plows, good roads, or school-houses. If the) Tj T*
(community were wealthy enough it would have more of these and of other) Tj T*
(things, and great as is the convenience of money, poorer communities) Tj T*
(have to do with little of it. It is, therefore, a confusion of cause and) Tj T*
(effect for poor communities to imagine that their poverty is due to lack) Tj T*
(of money.) Tj T*
