([Sidenote: More workers now in better-paid callings]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. _The increased proportion of workers in the higher occupations means) Tj T*
(a further rise in the average condition of the masses._ A smaller) Tj T*
(proportion of workers is now engaged in the low-paid industries than) Tj T*
(fifty years ago, and a correspondingly larger proportion is in the) Tj T*
(better, or highly paid, industries. Decade by decade the proportion) Tj T*
(shifts toward the upper part of the scale. Both in America and in) Tj T*
(England \(doubtless also in other countries\) more men are now engaged in) Tj T*
(the higher professions and skilled occupations, a smaller proportion in) Tj T*
(the lower occupations. This would raise the average of wages even if the) Tj T*
(wages of particular occupations had not risen.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: The masses gain by general social advance]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4. _The diffused advantages of progress mean relatively more to the) Tj T*
(masses than to the rich._ In the olden days the poor man was bound to) Tj T*
(the spot where he lived, the rich man had his carriage; to-day poor and) Tj T*
(rich ride side by side in the trolley car. The introduction of these) Tj T*
(cheap methods of enjoyment means relatively more to the poor. Better) Tj T*
(medical care, better sanitation, more abundant food, clothing, comfort,) Tj T*
(free schools, and libraries have all a part in this movement. The) Tj T*
(enormous possibilities in these lines are just beginning to be realized.) Tj T*
(The achievements of the last twenty years read like a story from) Tj T*
(fairy-land. It tells the leveling up of the conditions enjoyed by the) Tj T*
(common man.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Better social conditions must grow out of the wage system]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Improvement in the wage system]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(5. _Any sound method of improving social conditions must grow out of) Tj T*
(experience, not break with it._ Even if things were on the downward) Tj T*
(instead of the upward road there would be no excuse for wild) Tj T*
(speculation. The only rational way is to find what is good in what is,) Tj T*
(and build upon it. There can be no excuse for suggesting a method from) Tj T*
(imagination. Projects of social change must be tried by successful) Tj T*
(experiment, and gradually fitted to present needs. It is in this way) Tj T*
(that the higher forms of life have developed; it is in this way that) Tj T*
(social and political institutions have come into being. Things that work) Tj T*
(successfully first in a small way are worthy of trial on a larger scale.) Tj T*
(The wage system is a favorite object of attack for radical social) Tj T*
(reformers. It has many unlovely features and there are many individual) Tj T*
(cases of hardship. It may well be asked, What method shall be pursued to) Tj T*
(reform it? Its retention, however, is not inconsistent with very great) Tj T*
(changes in the present political and economic arrangements. The) Tj T*
(impersonal economic forces are working for improvement; but further,) Tj T*
(there is a growth of sentiment, an increase in sympathy, a feeling among) Tj T*
(men that the "cash nexus" is not the only bond that should unite) Tj T*
(different classes, and this sympathy is becoming an economic force,) Tj T*
(softening and improving many of the most unlovely features of the modern) Tj T*
(wage system.) Tj T*
