(§ II. EFFECT OF MACHINERY ON THE WELFARE AND WAGES OF THE MASSES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Evil of sudden introduction of machinery]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _The immediate effect of improved machinery, if suddenly introduced,) Tj T*
(is almost always to throw some men out of employment._ Any sudden change) Tj T*
(in industry injures men who have become adapted to the work that is) Tj T*
(affected. A well-mastered trade, a wage-earning though intangible) Tj T*
(possession, may be made suddenly valueless. Men cannot quickly change) Tj T*
(their methods of working or their place of work. This is as true of) Tj T*
(change brought about by new trade routes or by scientific discoveries) Tj T*
(\(where machinery does not enter in\) as in the case of labor-saving) Tj T*
(machines. If machines displace labor rapidly, men who cannot adjust) Tj T*
(themselves to the new conditions suffer, and there are always some who) Tj T*
(cannot adjust themselves, always some who suffer. It is rarely possible) Tj T*
(for a man past middle life to shift over into a new trade where his) Tj T*
(efficiency will be as great and his pay as high as in the old. New) Tj T*
(methods of puddling iron sent many old men into the poorhouses of) Tj T*
(Pennsylvania only a few years ago. Even where the total employment) Tj T*
(increases, the individual sometimes suffers. The increased demand) Tj T*
(resulting from the cheapening of a product may call for more workers) Tj T*
(than were employed before the new machinery came in, and yet some of the) Tj T*
(former workmen may be thrown out of employment. The introduction of the) Tj T*
(linotype is said to have displaced a large number of hand type-setters,) Tj T*
(but to have increased greatly the amount of printing. As the machines) Tj T*
(are expensive and cannot be worked properly by men not highly expert,) Tj T*
(men past thirty-five years of age have not been allowed to learn their) Tj T*
(use.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Loss falls on the less efficient workers]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The least efficient men in any trade always suffer most. The change) Tj T*
(crushes hardest the man at the margin of employment. The more skilled) Tj T*
(workman can hasten his pace and still earn a living wage in competition) Tj T*
(with a machine, while the less skilled can but drop out entirely,) Tj T*
(innocent victims of an economic change, sacrifices to the cause of) Tj T*
(industrial progress. Happily such pathetic incidents are relatively not) Tj T*
(numerous. Most machinery is introduced in commercial centers, and) Tj T*
(gradually spreads to other factories in such a way that most men can) Tj T*
(adapt themselves to the change. The effect of machinery must not be) Tj T*
(judged by the extreme cases. It was found that there were more) Tj T*
(hand-looms in use in England in 1850 than fifty years before, though in) Tj T*
(the meantime power-looms had displaced the hand-looms in all the great) Tj T*
(factories.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Error of the "lump of labor" notion]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _After time for adjustment, the total sum of employment is as great) Tj T*
(as before, but the labor is differently distributed._ The "lump of) Tj T*
(labor" idea, as it is called, is widely held, especially among) Tj T*
