([Sidenote: The growth of factories]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(5. _The early effects of the factory system on the health, intelligence,) Tj T*
(and morals of the workers often have been bad; but not necessarily the) Tj T*
(abiding effects._ Some kinds of machines can be more profitably used) Tj T*
(when they are grouped in great factories, and, where this is common, it) Tj T*
(is spoken of as the factory system. In the ideal modern factory) Tj T*
(\(realized in few cases\) each smaller machine is a part of a larger) Tj T*
(organization of machinery, so perfect that the material goes in at one) Tj T*
(end of the building and out at the other without the loss of a single) Tj T*
(motion. Factories compel great numbers of laborers to live near each) Tj T*
(other and to work together. The sudden crowding together of people into) Tj T*
(new social relations is usually bad for morals. Men are moral under the) Tj T*
(eyes of their neighbors, acquaintances, and families; habits become) Tj T*
(adjusted to right standards, and the temptations in new conditions are) Tj T*
(always great. Until of late, engineering science has not been able to) Tj T*
(deal with the problems that arise where population is densely crowded,) Tj T*
(and the early factories with their surroundings were most unsanitary.) Tj T*
(Under the degrading conditions that resulted in some places, especially) Tj T*
(in England, the effect of machinery on the intelligence of the workers) Tj T*
(was bad. Whether this is its natural result is debatable, but the) Tj T*
(factory worker in general does not appear to be less intelligent than) Tj T*
(the agricultural worker. The alertness of the city dweller is due) Tj T*
(doubtless to social contact more than to the immediate work he does.) Tj T*
(This work may or may not be less thought-awakening than work with simple) Tj T*
(tools. There is a general improvement along all the lines of) Tj T*
(intelligence, morals, and health. The conditions in the cities as) Tj T*
(regards health and morals are approaching those of agricultural) Tj T*
(communities. While many factory districts are forlorn, there may be seen) Tj T*
(around many factories more happy conditions, better buildings, better) Tj T*
(sanitation, increased leisure for workers, workmen's clubs, educational) Tj T*
(agencies, and many other evidences of civic and social progress.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Problems of large industry]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(6. _The great social consequences flowing from the concentration of) Tj T*
(industry and wealth are the most serious problems in the relation of) Tj T*
(machinery to labor._ The ownership of tools was widely diffused in) Tj T*
(medieval times. It is not yet evident how many can own a share in great) Tj T*
(factories, but the control drifts into few hands. It is not yet clear) Tj T*
(what social effects great corporations will have on our democratic) Tj T*
(institutions. Many problems of large industry remain to be solved in the) Tj T*
(near future. The question in the old form, as to the effect of machinery) Tj T*
(on labor, is no longer open. It has been clearly answered by experience) Tj T*
(and explained by theory: the economic effect of machinery is to lift the) Tj T*
(productiveness and efficiency of the average man. The benefits are) Tj T*
(unequally distributed, but nearly all share in them to some degree. The) Tj T*
(question which the future will have to answer is, What will be the) Tj T*
