(§ I. THE OBJECTS OF TRADE-UNIONS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Definition and purposes of trade-unions]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _A trade-union is an association of wage-workers for purposes of) Tj T*
(mutual information, mutual help, and for the raising of wages._ The term) Tj T*
(trade-union is used in a general sense both of combinations of workers) Tj T*
(in the same trade, and of men in different trades, though usually the) Tj T*
(latter are called _labor_-unions. The "Knights of Labor" is a good) Tj T*
(example of the labor-union, the "American Federation of Labor" of a) Tj T*
(combination of trade-unions. The Knights of Labor is composed of local) Tj T*
(branches to which workers of every class except lawyers and) Tj T*
(saloon-keepers are admitted. The Federation of Labor, however, is) Tj T*
(composed of chapters, or lodges, that are homogeneous, all the men of) Tj T*
(each lodge being in the same trade.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The definition given is broad enough to include the various degrees of) Tj T*
(help given and the various methods adopted by trade-unions to accomplish) Tj T*
(their objects. Trade-unions are mutual-benefit associations: insurance) Tj T*
(against accident, sickness, death, or lack of employment, forms an) Tj T*
(important part, and in some cases almost the whole of their work. All) Tj T*
(unions in a measure serve their members as employment bureaus, while in) Tj T*
(some unions this is a most important feature. Through trade-papers,) Tj T*
(correspondence, and personal meetings, information is exchanged) Tj T*
(regarding trade conditions, and great mutual service is thus rendered.) Tj T*
(But a great deal of the help given is in the more impersonal economic) Tj T*
(ways: help to get from the employers better wages, to secure shorter) Tj T*
(hours, to improve in various ways the conditions of employment.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Lack of personal touch between employers and workmen]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _The organization of workers has resulted from the separation of the) Tj T*
(economic and personal interests of employers and workmen._ The control) Tj T*
(of industry has become more concentrated during the age of machinery,) Tj T*
(and this has reduced the feeling of economic unity among the different) Tj T*
(ranks of industry. There is now to the average workman no possibility of) Tj T*
(becoming a master, an employer. The largeness of industry forbids,) Tj T*
(moreover, the meeting and personal acquaintance of employer and workman) Tj T*
(which were before possible. Misunderstandings grow when men cannot talk) Tj T*
(over their differences. The social chasm has widened between the workmen) Tj T*
(and the responsible director of industry. As a result of these changes,) Tj T*
(the attitude of the employer very often has become that of the buyer of) Tj T*
(labor as a mere ware. He has with the mass of his employees no personal) Tj T*
(relations whatever. Under these conditions, when the employer feels the) Tj T*
