(§ I. THE NATURE OF PRODUCTION) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Man's active intervention in production here to be studied]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _The aim of industrial effort is the increase of the quantity and) Tj T*
(quality of scarce goods; this is economic production._ The thought has) Tj T*
(become familiar to the student that the supply of economic resources of) Tj T*
(whatever sort is limited, while the wants are practically unlimited. A) Tj T*
(supply of consumption goods meets a perennial stream of wants, the) Tj T*
(result being that value is attributed to things. The aim of production) Tj T*
(is to add to scarce things, to make the supply of goods as large as) Tj T*
(possible. There is occasion here to recall the thought of the two) Tj T*
(aspects of production noticed in Chapter 24. Man's part in production is) Tj T*
(passive when goods come into existence without his effort. One can) Tj T*
(imagine the indolent savage of the tropics, lying under the banana-tree,) Tj T*
(letting the fruit drop into his mouth. One can conceive of a tribe) Tj T*
(living upon manna, where every day the people awoke to discover a) Tj T*
(certain amount of food provided to each person's hand. Though no effort) Tj T*
(could increase that amount, still, if the food differed in flavor and) Tj T*
(the better qualities were rare, value would come into existence and) Tj T*
(exchange would arise. Now there is something very analogous to that in) Tj T*
(daily experience. There are some goods which effort can do little to,) Tj T*
(increase. Usually, however, there is a possibility of change and) Tj T*
(adaptation to make them better suited to needs, and there is required) Tj T*
(the use of intelligence to choose among the goods and to employ them in) Tj T*
(the best way. Further, man can intervene and direct the course of) Tj T*
(industry; he does not merely gather what is provided. It is this active) Tj T*
(intervention and effort that is here to be considered.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: The four essential characteristics of value]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _To have value, a thing must be of the right stuff, in the right) Tj T*
(form, at the right time, and at the right place to gratify wants._ A) Tj T*
(distinction is sometimes made between elemental, form, time, and place) Tj T*
(value. It is a mistake to say that the value of anything is due to any) Tj T*
(one of these features, for to have value all must be united in a single) Tj T*
(thing. But the distinction is useful in emphasizing the missing) Tj T*
(characteristics, which if supplied, cause value to emerge. Ice may be) Tj T*
(considered to have form value when produced artificially by a machine,) Tj T*
(time value when stored from winter to summer, and place value when) Tj T*
(brought from the north to the south. But not less essential is the) Tj T*
(psychological condition of a hungry and thirsty population ready to) Tj T*
(consume the ice. Any act or agent is said to be productive which works) Tj T*
(in any one of these respects: puts things in better form, or in a more) Tj T*
(fitting place, or provides them at a more fitting time to serve human) Tj T*
(wants.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Economic vs. technical changes in goods]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. _Economic production \(in contrast with technical or merely formal) Tj T*
(production\) is such a change in goods as is attended by an increase in) Tj T*
(value._ It is often well to contrast form, appearance, imitation, with) Tj T*
