(§ II. COMBINATION OF THE FACTORS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: The factors of production defined]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _The various parts, materials, and agents that unite to form products) Tj T*
(are called the factors of production._ In a general sense every separate) Tj T*
(thing that enters into industry is a factor; as, in agriculture, for) Tj T*
(example, the seed, plows, fields, fences, barns, cattle, labor. But) Tj T*
(usually in economic discussion, these numerous factors are grouped in) Tj T*
(large classes. The main factors are two, variously named as man and) Tj T*
(nature, or labor and material agents, or humanity and wealth. Rejecting,) Tj T*
(as we have, the old view as to the nature of consumption goods and as to) Tj T*
(the nature and possibility of the distinction between "land" and) Tj T*
(artificial capital, we class under wealth all material economic agents) Tj T*
(whatsoever. The discussion of labor and wages has broadly laid down the) Tj T*
(principles that apply to the value of human effort, but the factor of) Tj T*
(directing energy presents in modern society so many important features) Tj T*
(that it calls for special and fuller consideration.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Progressive stages of control over natural conditions]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _The economic progress of society has been marked by decreasing) Tj T*
(dependence on the bounties and chances of nature and by increasing) Tj T*
(control of natural forces by man._ Various stages of progress in human) Tj T*
(history have been recognized. First is the stage of _appropriation_--the) Tj T*
(stage of hunting, or of fishing, or of gathering fruits. Man in this) Tj T*
(stage is still an animal in his economic methods, not guiding and) Tj T*
(controlling nature, but merely gathering what nature chances to bring) Tj T*
(forth. The limitations to man's powers in this stage are marked. There) Tj T*
(is excess of supply and waste at one season, scarcity and great) Tj T*
(suffering at another. With such crude utilization of the bounties of) Tj T*
(nature, a vast area will support but a small population. When sheep and) Tj T*
(cattle have been domesticated, and where there is a large area for) Tj T*
(grazing, industry rises to the _pastoral_ stage. While still dependent) Tj T*
(on nature's bounties for the feeding of his cattle, man is hourly) Tj T*
(intervening to increase, regulate, and improve the supply of food and) Tj T*
(materials. Famines are more rare, economic welfare is greater, a greater) Tj T*
