(§ I. THE DIRECTION OF INDUSTRY) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Judgment and self-direction as elements in personal skill]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _In the simplest kinds of individual production the value of the) Tj T*
(results depends largely on intelligent choice._ Even for the solitary) Tj T*
(worker the choice of the right time to do work is most important. The) Tj T*
(first thing Robinson Crusoe did was to turn to the ship to save as much) Tj T*
(as possible of the cargo before it was dashed in pieces by the waves. If) Tj T*
(he had begun first to till the soil to provide a future supply of food) Tj T*
(it would have shown one kind of foresight, but it would have shown very) Tj T*
(poor judgment. Every moment of delay in recovering the cargo of the) Tj T*
(wrecked vessel cost him many useful materials. The humblest farmer has a) Tj T*
(great range of choice and a need of good judgment in fixing the time to) Tj T*
(sow, to reap, to do each simple task. There is the same need to-day for) Tj T*
(the small shopkeepers, for the blacksmiths, for the small producers of) Tj T*
(all kinds to make wise choice of time in the use of their own labor.) Tj T*
(There is also a wide range of choice in the distributing and combining) Tj T*
(of labor, agents, and materials. A limited supply of agents can be used) Tj T*
(to secure a variety of goods, more or less desirable. There are many) Tj T*
(chances for mistake, but in the long run it is judgment, not chance,) Tj T*
(that determines the success of one man as compared with another. There) Tj T*
(is a choice in ways and methods by which a thing can be done. There are) Tj T*
(many wrong ways, there is but one best way, at any stage of industrial) Tj T*
(progress. While most work is done in customary ways and little) Tj T*
(independent judgment is required, yet in every business from time to) Tj T*
(time new problems arise and call for an exercise of choice as to) Tj T*
(methods. Moral qualities are continually called for, such as control of) Tj T*
(impulse, and the giving up of the comfort of the moment. The wisdom of) Tj T*
(our fathers is embodied in a multitude of proverbs that suggest the wise) Tj T*
(course. Men must "make hay while the sun shines," not lie in the shade.) Tj T*
(But virtue fails less often from lack of knowledge than from lack of) Tj T*
(will. As men differ in judgment, character, and will-power, their) Tj T*
(products differ, even in the simplest circumstances. The ability to) Tj T*
(choose and to do wisely is an element in personal skill.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Direction of a group of workers]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
