(exceptional kind of scarcity, scarcity due in part to some source or) Tj T*
(cause not ordinarily present. It is a bad practice in definition to) Tj T*
(apply two words to one idea, leaving the other idea unnamed, as is done) Tj T*
(when monopoly is made synonymous with scarcity. Both words are needed.) Tj T*
(Such a usage unfortunately is common in economic literature. Many) Tj T*
(economic writers, for example, have called landownership monopoly,) Tj T*
(saying that land being the work of nature cannot be increased by men,) Tj T*
(and therefore must always be scarce. Even if it were true that in the) Tj T*
(economic sense land could be produced by man, there still would be) Tj T*
(confusion here between a general class of goods and a special thing. The) Tj T*
(fact that a particular field cannot be duplicated does not make a) Tj T*
(monopoly of land as a whole, any more than the existence of desert land) Tj T*
(in Arizona makes land valueless or a free good. Nor is a land-owner a) Tj T*
(monopolist any more than is the owner of a valuable machine. The owner) Tj T*
(of forty acres of land worth four hundred dollars, or the owner of a) Tj T*
(village lot worth a hundred dollars, can hardly be called a monopolist.) Tj T*
(It leads to absurdity to use the word monopoly with reference to) Tj T*
(landownership indiscriminately. Neither mere scarcity nor the limitation) Tj T*
(of natural stores should be called monopoly when ownership is scattered) Tj T*
(and combination between owners does not exist.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
