([Sidenote: Forces governing competitive prices]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _A competitive producer gets the highest price that will permit him) Tj T*
(to dispose of his product._ The enterpriser seeks to get the highest) Tj T*
(price for his product that the market will afford. His ability to) Tj T*
(continue making a profit at a lower price does not induce him to reduce) Tj T*
(the price unless the reduction is to his interest. The ordinary) Tj T*
(competing manufacturer is limited in his price by two things: first,) Tj T*
(his customers may cease to buy such articles entirely and may substitute) Tj T*
(other goods if the price is too high; secondly, they may buy of other) Tj T*
(sellers. Between his wish to keep the price up, and the customer's wish) Tj T*
(to buy as cheaply as he can, the price is fixed at a point where there) Tj T*
(is no inducement for others to come in and reduce his sales, or for him) Tj T*
(to seek a better market. There may be under these conditions a potential) Tj T*
(but very limited monopoly power. The sole druggist in a small town might) Tj T*
(occasionally get extortionate prices from particular customers in times) Tj T*
(of dire need, but he would thus drive away much of his custom, and would) Tj T*
(tempt a fairer and less grasping competitor to come in. Thus, when men) Tj T*
(and capital are free to come and go, there results an average or normal) Tj T*
(return for ability and agents of a certain grade. Prices come to) Tj T*
(equilibrium where each is selling his total product.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Monopoly's greater control of price]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
