(legitimate economies of large production._ The economies that are) Tj T*
(possible within a single factory may be still greater in a number of) Tj T*
(combined or federated industries. The cost of management, amount of) Tj T*
(stock carried, advertising, cost of selling the product, may all be) Tj T*
(smaller per unit of product. A large aggregation can control credit) Tj T*
(better and escape loss from bad debts. By regulating and equalizing the) Tj T*
(output in the different localities, it can run more nearly full time.) Tj T*
(Being acquainted with the entire situation, it can reduce the friction.) Tj T*
(A strong combination has advantages in shipment. It can have a) Tj T*
(clearing-house for orders and ship from the nearest source of supply.) Tj T*
(The least efficient factories can be first closed when demand falls off.) Tj T*
(Factories can be specialized to produce that for which each is best) Tj T*
(fitted. The magnitude of the industry and its presence in different) Tj T*
(localities strengthens its influence with the railroads. Its political) Tj T*
(as well as its economic power is increased.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Integration of industry]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A recent phase of corporate growth is the "integration of industry,") Tj T*
(that is, the grouping under one control of a whole series of industries.) Tj T*
(One company may carry the iron ore through all the processes from the) Tj T*
(mine to the finished product. A railroad line across the continent owns) Tj T*
(its own steamers for shipping goods to Asia or Europe. Large wholesale) Tj T*
(houses own or control the output of entire factories. The possibilities) Tj T*
(in this direction have only begun to be realized.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Combination prevents competition]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. _The men uniting to form a trust always declare that its formation) Tj T*
(is the necessary result of excessive competition._ The statement is) Tj T*
(often true in the sense that a hard fight and lower prices have preceded) Tj T*
(the formation of the trust. But as this excessive competition usually is) Tj T*
(for the very purpose of forcing the combination, this explanation is a) Tj T*
(begging of the question. It is fallacious also in that it ignores the) Tj T*
(marginal principle in the problem of profits. Profits are never) Tj T*
(homogeneous from factory to factory, and to those that are on the margin) Tj T*
(competition may appear excessive. It is generally the largest and) Tj T*
(strongest factories, in the more favored situations, that, in order to) Tj T*
(get rid of troublesome competitors, force the smaller, weaker,) Tj T*
(industries to come into the trust. When, therefore, it is said that) Tj T*
(competition is destructive, it may be a partial truth, but more likely) Tj T*
(it is a pleasantry reflecting the happy humor of the prosperous) Tj T*
(promoters of the combination.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Financial gains of combination]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4. _Another strong motive for the combination is the profit to promoters) Tj T*
(and organizers._ There are indirect as well as direct gains to the) Tj T*
(managers of a large business. There is the gain from the production and) Tj T*
(sale of goods to consumers, and there is the gain from the financial) Tj T*
(management, from the rise and fall in the value of stock. The promoters) Tj T*
(of a combination often expect to make from sales to the investing public) Tj T*
