([Sidenote: Economics of the trust problem]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _The economist's task, strictly confined, is to explain the relation) Tj T*
(of trusts to prices, not to solve the problem of their political) Tj T*
(control._ The question of trusts is such a large one that its discussion) Tj T*
(here must be confined to those aspects having close relation to the) Tj T*
(central subject of economic study,--the laws of value. These laws were) Tj T*
(by the older economists thought to be true only within the limits of) Tj T*
(free competition. Seeing that in various ways this freedom is interfered) Tj T*
(with not only by caste, custom, organized labor, but by patents,) Tj T*
(political privileges, and the power of large aggregations of capital \(in) Tj T*
(short by all things that check the flow of ability and of agents from) Tj T*
(one industry to another\), the question occurs: Are the abstract laws of) Tj T*
(rents, profits, and wages of any significance or of any help in) Tj T*
(discussing the great practical questions of to-day? Are not prices) Tj T*
(determined by the personal whim of industrial despots who can bid) Tj T*
(defiance to the laws of price? The control of trusts by legislative) Tj T*
(action is largely a political problem, but it must be guided by a) Tj T*
(correct economic analysis. Proposed legislative measures often assume or) Tj T*
(imply that in no way, directly or indirectly, is competition found in) Tj T*
(the problem. It should be the aim of economic study to make clear the) Tj T*
(true bearing and force of monopoly power in practical problems of value.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
