(citizens. The power of directors and of other officers to manipulate) Tj T*
(earnings and dividends, and thus to affect the market value of the) Tj T*
(stock, leaves the investing public helpless. The practice by officials) Tj T*
(in great corporations of speculating in their own stocks, whose prices) Tj T*
(they can manipulate, is so common as scarcely to attract comment. Large) Tj T*
(fortunes result from this betrayal of the trust imposed by the) Tj T*
(shareholders. This is not legitimate speculation; it is like loading the) Tj T*
(dice, pulling the horse, drugging the pugilist--things despised and) Tj T*
(condemned even in gambling and sporting circles.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Two types of speculation]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It appears, therefore, that in the complex conditions of modern business) Tj T*
(there is a legitimate concentration of risk in the more capable hands,) Tj T*
(but also a growth of opportunities for illegitimate speculation and for) Tj T*
(large dishonest gains that were not possible before. These two types of) Tj T*
(speculation should be distinguished, as far as possible, in thought and) Tj T*
(in practice; but this it not easy in concrete instances, which vary) Tj T*
(almost indistinguishably from the clear case of honest earnings to the) Tj T*
(other extreme of illegitimate gains.) Tj T*
