(CHAPTER 37) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(CRISES AND INDUSTRIAL DEPRESSIONS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(§ I. DEFINITION AND DESCRIPTION OF CRISES) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Broader definition of a crisis]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _In a broad sense, a crisis is a decisive moment or turning point;) Tj T*
(hence, in industry, a collapse of prosperity._ In the course of a fever) Tj T*
(the crisis is the point where there is a turn for the better or for the) Tj T*
(worse. The figure of speech as applied to industrial conditions would) Tj T*
(seem to fail, in that what precedes is apparently exuberant health, not) Tj T*
(disease. Business conditions do not move along uniformly. There are) Tj T*
(waves of prosperity. Profits are apparently great, then may be suddenly) Tj T*
(swept away. The profits of the prosperous time are partly illusory, or) Tj T*
(exist only on paper. The situation has all the unhealthiness of the) Tj T*
(fever-patient. Men trade in promises and when the crisis comes, they) Tj T*
(have only promises for profits. The discussion of business management) Tj T*
(and profits is not complete without a consideration of this rhythmic) Tj T*
(movement of confidence and prices.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A crisis in the business affairs of an individual, in the sense of a) Tj T*
(collapse of prosperity, may occur from many mischances. A local crisis) Tj T*
(may be felt in some one neighborhood as a result of flood, of fire, or) Tj T*
(of other accidents. Such a case was that which occurred in 1864, in) Tj T*
(Manchester, England, when the cotton factories were compelled to close) Tj T*
(because the supply of cotton was cut off by the blockade of the ports of) Tj T*
(the South in the Civil War. Such a local crisis sometimes results from a) Tj T*
(change of transportation, throwing a town out of the line of trade.) Tj T*
(These have been mentioned in discussing chance and risk; but the) Tj T*
(phenomenon known generally as an industrial crisis is of wider extent) Tj T*
(and of a more peculiar nature.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Various types of crises]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _In a more special sense a financial crisis is the confusion and loss) Tj T*
(that mark the end of a period of rising prices; an industrial depression) Tj T*
(is the period of hard times that follows._ The word crisis suggests a) Tj T*
(brief period, a moment, something that is severe, sudden, and soon over.) Tj T*
(The term financial panic is frequently used as a synonym for financial) Tj T*
(crisis. A crisis in the narrower sense has to do with prices--is always) Tj T*
(connected with money in some way. While, therefore, crises may be) Tj T*
(divided into industrial, speculative, and financial, according to their) Tj T*
(immediate occasion, all of them are financial in the sense that they) Tj T*
(have to do with a change in the general price level. A crisis is a jolt) Tj T*
(to prices which shatters the credit of some banks, brokers, merchants,) Tj T*
ET
