BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(and manufacturers. Crises are thus peculiar to the money economy and to) Tj T*
(a developed industry. Not every business misfortune is to be called an) Tj T*
(industrial crisis, but only those where prices and credit are generally) Tj T*
(depressed. A long period of hard times is sometimes called a crisis, but) Tj T*
(it is better to distinguish it by the term industrial depression.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Industrial conditions preceding a crisis]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. _The period leading up to a crisis is one of general prosperity._) Tj T*
(Industry in successive decades does not pass through an unvarying series) Tj T*
(of changes, but history repeats itself with sufficient regularity to) Tj T*
(justify the view that a certain series of changes is typical in modern) Tj T*
(industry. When prices are at the lowest point many factories are closed,) Tj T*
(and much labor is unemployed. Conditions are worse in some industries) Tj T*
(than in others. General economy and great caution prevail; few new) Tj T*
(enterprises are undertaken. To those having available money this is a) Tj T*
(good time to buy, and property begins to change hands. Then hoarded) Tj T*
(money begins to come out of its hiding-places. Money flows in from other) Tj T*
(countries, particularly if business conditions are better abroad than) Tj T*
(here, for low prices make a country a good place in which to buy. At the) Tj T*
(same time that the money in circulation thus increases, there is a) Tj T*
(general return of confidence that increases credit. Not only are there) Tj T*
(more dollars, but each does more work. Then old enterprises are resumed) Tj T*
(and new ones are undertaken. The purchase of materials in larger) Tj T*
(quantities causes a rise in prices and an increase in costs. The surplus) Tj T*
(labor on the margin of efficiency gets employment, and wages begin to) Tj T*
(increase. The only classes not sharing in this improvement are the) Tj T*
(receivers of fixed incomes. As prices rise, the purchasing power of) Tj T*
(their incomes gradually falls.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: The crisis and its results]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4. _The crisis is a moment of widespread loss, which is followed by a) Tj T*
(long period of small profits to most enterprises, and of enforced) Tj T*
(economy._ As prices cease to go up rapidly, the question arises in many) Tj T*
(minds whether the movement can continue, and if not, when it will cease.) Tj T*
(Men wish to hold on for the last profits, and are willing to risk) Tj T*
(something to gain them. When foreign prices do not rise in as great) Tj T*
(proportion as domestic prices, foreign imports are stimulated and the) Tj T*
(quantity of exports falls. This disturbs the equilibrium of money and) Tj T*
(requires at length large and continued exportation of specie. This) Tj T*
(checks prices, and, reducing the specie reserves of the banks, compels) Tj T*
(them to be more cautious. The fall in the value of many stocks and) Tj T*
(securities held by the banks forces many brokers and speculators to) Tj T*
(convert their resources into ready money. This is the moment of danger;) Tj T*
(weak enterprises find their foundations crumbling, and there are many) Tj T*
(failures. The falling prices, the shattered credit, and the financial) Tj T*
(losses force many factories to close; many workmen are thrown out of) Tj T*
(employment, and business must again enter upon a period of retrenchment,) Tj T*
(for it has completed the cycle of changing prices.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
