(§ II. CRISES IN THE NINETEENTH CENTURY) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: No financial crises in the Middle Ages]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _The periods of industrial hardship in the Middle Ages were connected) Tj T*
(with adverse conditions of production, not with the collapse of prices._) Tj T*
(Periods of exceptional hardship in medieval times were mostly due to) Tj T*
(political oppression, famine, wars, pestilence, and scourges of nature.) Tj T*
(There being very little of the money economy, there was no development) Tj T*
(of credit and of credit prices. The money economy began, as has been) Tj T*
(noted, in the cities. As the use of money spread, as larger commercial) Tj T*
(enterprises were undertaken, as borrowing and the payment of interest) Tj T*
(became common, there began to appear in city trading circles, on a small) Tj T*
(scale, the phenomena of the modern crisis.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: European crises of the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _In Europe general industrial crises date from 1763 and have occurred) Tj T*
(at more or less regular intervals since._ It frequently is said that the) Tj T*
(cycle, or period, of crises is ten years, but it takes an elastic) Tj T*
(imagination to find support for this in history. The crises of the) Tj T*
(eighteenth century occurred in 1763, 1783, 1793, these dates marking the) Tj T*
(close of wars of some magnitude. The crises were not widespread or) Tj T*
(general, but were more marked in England, which was most developed) Tj T*
(industrially and in its money economy. Likewise in the nineteenth) Tj T*
(century, the crises were of unequal force in the various countries,) Tj T*
(usually being severer in England. The English crises may be roughly) Tj T*
(dated 1803, 1825, 1838, 1847, 1857, 1864, 1875, 1890. These were) Tj T*
(attributed to various causes; that of 1825 to over-trading abroad; that) Tj T*
(of 1847 to railroad-building; that of 1864 to the interruption of the) Tj T*
(cotton trade and of commerce, as a result of the Civil War in America.) Tj T*
(While in many parts of England the crisis of 1864 was unusually severe,) Tj T*
(in other countries it was of little moment. Germany, after several years) Tj T*
(of great speculative prosperity, had a most severe crisis in 1875; while) Tj T*
(France \(a somewhat significant fact\), although prostrated by the war of) Tj T*
(1870-71, losing a large amount of wealth, and paying a thousand millions) Tj T*
(of dollars to Germany as a war indemnity, escaped a commercial crisis) Tj T*
(almost entirely at that time.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Crises in the United States]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. _In the United States there have been five marked crises: the first) Tj T*
(in 1817, the last in 1893._ These crises were of date 1817-20, 1837-39,) Tj T*
(1857, 1873, 1893. Major crises thus occurred about twenty years apart,) Tj T*
(and minor crises in several instances alternated with them, notably in) Tj T*
(1866, 1884, and we might add, 1903. These crises were the culmination of) Tj T*
(different kinds of speculation, usually spoken of as their causes. The) Tj T*
(crisis of 1817 was due to over-trading and to the immense importation) Tj T*
(following the war of 1812 and the resumption of commerce with Europe in) Tj T*
(1816. In 1837-39 came in quick succession two crises, not quite distinct) Tj T*
(from each other, the second similar to the relapse of a fever patient.) Tj T*
(The immediate occasions were over-speculation in lands, a great issue of) Tj T*
