(conditions, on the whole, tended for a while toward the worse until the) Tj T*
(spring of 1904.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: General features of crises]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4. _Irregular in time, and unlike in their immediate occasions, crises) Tj T*
(show some general features._ The chief of these are told in the brief) Tj T*
(story of the course of prices. Crises are less severe in countries with) Tj T*
(less developed money and credit systems. They are harder in the United) Tj T*
(States and England than in Germany, harder in Germany than in France,) Tj T*
(harder in western Europe than in eastern Europe, harder in Christendom) Tj T*
(than in heathendom. They are less severe in rural districts, where) Tj T*
(prosperity depends more on crop conditions, and business has in it less) Tj T*
(of financial speculation. Their effects are least felt in the staple) Tj T*
(industries, for when hard times come, people economize on the less) Tj T*
(essential things. The glove-factory, the silk-factory, the) Tj T*
(golf-club-factory are more likely to close than the flouring-mill. They) Tj T*
