() Tj T*
(2. _Another group of theories explains the crises as being due to money,) Tj T*
(either too much or too little._ The unregulated issue of bank-notes has) Tj T*
(been assigned as the cause of crises, especially under the circumstances) Tj T*
(accompanying such crises as those of 1837 and 1857 in America, when) Tj T*
(bank-note issues chanced to be the agency most marked in the undue and) Tj T*
(unsound expansion of credit. The issue of government paper money,) Tj T*
(leading to inflation and speculation, is assigned as a cause leading up) Tj T*
(to such a crisis as that of 1873, following our Civil War. The reverse) Tj T*
(view is taken by the advocates of a cheap and plentiful money. They say) Tj T*
(that these crises were caused, not by the expansion, but by the) Tj T*
(reduction of bank-notes; for example, not by the inflation of prices) Tj T*
(through the issue of greenbacks in 1862 to 1865, but by the contraction) Tj T*
(of the currency from 1866 to 1873.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Their inadequacy]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There is only a fragment of truth in these various views. It is always) Tj T*
(lack of money at the moment of the crisis that causes any particular) Tj T*
(failure, and in that sense it is always lack of money that causes a) Tj T*
(crisis. But the question is, whether in any reasonable sense it can be) Tj T*
(said that it was lack of a circulating medium before the crisis that) Tj T*
(brought it on. There is no support for this view, except in the rare) Tj T*
(case when the money standard is undergoing a rapid change, as in the) Tj T*
(United States from 1866 to 1873, and the statement then needs much) Tj T*
(modification and explanation. The money theories of crises are nearer to) Tj T*
(the truth than are the over-production type, for the crisis is always) Tj T*
(connected with money and prices. But it cannot be said that the absolute) Tj T*
(amount of money in circulation in the period preceding crises gives) Tj T*
(occasion to them. In a few instances a rapid change in the amount has) Tj T*
(had an important effect, but this fact does not explain crises in) Tj T*
(general.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Lack of confidence is said to be a cause of crises. This is a truism,) Tj T*
(but the lack of confidence is not without reason and cause.) Tj T*
(Over-confidence in the period of expanding prices is succeeded by) Tj T*
(extreme depression when many false hopes are shattered.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Capitalization theory of crises]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. _Crises must be explained essentially as the forcible and sudden) Tj T*
(movement of readjustment in the mistaken capitalization of productive) Tj T*
(agents._ Capitalization runs through all industry. The value of) Tj T*
(everything that lasts for more than a moment is built in part upon rents) Tj T*
(that are not actual, but expectative, whose amount, therefore, is a) Tj T*
(matter of guesswork, or "speculation." Many unknown factors enter into) Tj T*
(the estimate of future rents. The universal tendency to rhythm in motion) Tj T*
(\(material or psychic\) manifests itself in an overestimate or) Tj T*
(underestimate of rent and of every other factor in value. This is) Tj T*
(emphasized by a psychological factor called the "hypnotism of the) Tj T*
(crowd," Most men follow a leader in investment as in other things. The) Tj T*
(spirit of speculation grows till it becomes almost a frenzy, and people) Tj T*
