(may be, inherit property when there is no will. When a miser dies in) Tj T*
(solitude and neglect, the world must be searched over to find a remote) Tj T*
(cousin to take the hoarded wealth. Inheritance is limited largely at) Tj T*
(present by the power of taxation. The view is growing that the claims of) Tj T*
(the society in which wealth has been acquired are stronger than those of) Tj T*
(relatives distant alike in space, in blood, and in affectionate) Tj T*
(interest. This view is reflected in many recent inheritance-tax laws) Tj T*
(which take from the shares of distant relatives a goodly portion for) Tj T*
(public purposes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The question is raised in many minds, If private property is not an) Tj T*
(absolute right, what shall be its limits? What changes should be made in) Tj T*
(it? The essential thought in the various attacks on the institution of) Tj T*
(private property is that, because it occasions inequality in incomes, it) Tj T*
(is not socially expedient. The conviction is growing that, in some) Tj T*
(general way, incomes should correspond to, and reflect, social service.) Tj T*
(It is well to consider more closely what the terms social expediency and) Tj T*
(social service imply.) Tj T*
