(§ I. INCOME FROM PROPERTY) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: The justice of property questioned]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _Property rights must meet the test of social expediency._ If private) Tj T*
(property is defended on the ground of social expediency, it must show) Tj T*
(good social results. It is not a sacred thing; it is open to) Tj T*
(examination, and must be judged by its fruits. Of all the forms of) Tj T*
(income, that from property has been most strongly attacked. The thought) Tj T*
(is that enjoyment of wealth should not be found apart from labor, and) Tj T*
(that it should bear some proportion to services performed. The enjoyment) Tj T*
(of an ample income by one who does no more than to draw checks or to) Tj T*
(sign coupons seems to many minds to be unjust; and it is often) Tj T*
(questioned whether there is any social service performed by the) Tj T*
(receivers of the rent from land. Property seems in many cases to be) Tj T*
(distributed without rule or reason. It does not correspond with beauty,) Tj T*
(strength, wit, wisdom, temperance, gentleness, or charity. Since the) Tj T*
(beginning of the Christian era, reformers have assailed and preached) Tj T*
(against the prevailing inequality of wealth. The idea that incomes, if) Tj T*
(not equal, should correspond to social service has always been present) Tj T*
(in some vague way in the minds of men.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Social effect of the right to give]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _The right to transmit property by inheritance or by gift may be) Tj T*
(judged with reference to its effect on the giver, on the receiver, and) Tj T*
(on society at large._ It is well to take these three points of view. The) Tj T*
(right to dispose of property either during life or at death has) Tj T*
(undoubtedly in many ways a good effect on the character of men. It) Tj T*
(stimulates the father to provide for his children, the husband to) Tj T*
(provide for his wife. There is a joy in giving, a joy in the power to) Tj T*
(bestow one's wealth on those one loves. The right to give stimulates) Tj T*
(industry, frugality, ingenuity, and yields productive results. Much of) Tj T*
(the existing wealth probably never would have been created if men did) Tj T*
(not have this right of gift. But there is a limit to the working of this) Tj T*
(motive, and other motives often are much more effective. Many men after) Tj T*
(gaining a competence continue to work for love of wealth and power in) Tj T*
(their own lifetime, as the miser continues to toil for love of gold.) Tj T*
(When men without families die wealthy, when men that have not the) Tj T*
(slightest interest in their nearest relatives labor and amass wealth) Tj T*
(till their dying day, it is evident that the right to bequeath property) Tj T*
(has little to do with their efforts. Love of accumulation and love of) Tj T*
(power in these cases supply the motive. A more limited liberty to) Tj T*
(dispose of property at death might still suffice, therefore, to call out) Tj T*
(the greater part of the efforts now made to accumulate property.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Effect of the right to receive]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(That the effects on the receiver of the property are good is somewhat) Tj T*
(more doubtful. It is true that children raised in great comfort or) Tj T*
(luxury would be more than ordinarily unhappy if plunged into poverty or) Tj T*
(even into humble circumstances on the death of their parents. There is) Tj T*
