(have chances they themselves did not enjoy. The greater social loss is) Tj T*
(not the dissipated fortunes, but the ruined characters.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Broader social effects of inheritance]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The effects of inheritance on the community are good in so far as it) Tj T*
(secures efficient management of wealth. If the son or relative has been) Tj T*
(in business with the deceased, there is a reason that he should inherit) Tj T*
(the property, and his succession to it makes the least disturbance to) Tj T*
(existing business conditions. But every profligate son is an argument) Tj T*
(against inheritance; every incompetent heir is an argument in the hands) Tj T*
(of the enemies of the existing order of society. It is to society's) Tj T*
(interest that no able-bodied member shall stand idle. Every child should) Tj T*
(have presented to him the motive to devote his powers to the social) Tj T*
(welfare in economic or other directions. Moreover, many feel that the) Tj T*
(great fortunes now accumulating through successive generations in the) Tj T*
(hands of a few families are endangering our free society, even if these) Tj T*
(fortunes should continue to be well administered. There is a widespread) Tj T*
(feeling that the heredity of great wealth is, like the heredity of) Tj T*
(political power, out of harmony with the democratic spirit--though this) Tj T*
(may easily become a misleading comparison. Still, democracy wishes to) Tj T*
(see men as individuals put to the test, not profiting forever by the) Tj T*
(deeds of their forebears. This feeling is shared by those who cannot be) Tj T*
(charged with radical prejudices. A few years ago the Illinois Bar) Tj T*
(Association passed a somewhat startling resolution favoring moderate) Tj T*
(limits to inherited fortunes. Every year sees bills of this purport) Tj T*
(introduced in the legislatures and in Congress. Andrew Carnegie says it) Tj T*
(would be a good thing if every boy had to start in poverty and make his) Tj T*
(own way. Cecil Rhodes recorded in his will his contempt for the idle,) Tj T*
(expectant heir.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: The test of wise inheritance laws]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. _Social expediency will limit the right of intestate inheritance to) Tj T*
(persons in essential economic and social relations._ Public opinion is) Tj T*
(not yet crystallized in favor of this formal proposition, but tends) Tj T*
(strongly toward it. The foregoing considerations show that the right of) Tj T*
(gift in the lifetime of the giver should be the freest. The right of) Tj T*
(bequest, that is, of gift by will, should be liberal. The man who has) Tj T*
(acquired wealth may well be trusted to decide who bear to him a close) Tj T*
