() Tj T*
(4. _Custom ruled a large share of the industrial life of the Middle) Tj T*
(Ages._ Political and economic interests were not clearly divided in the) Tj T*
(Middle Ages. Land was the all-important kind of wealth. Military and) Tj T*
(other public services were performed by the vassal, who thus at the same) Tj T*
(time paid his taxes and the rent of the land. The landlord was at once) Tj T*
(the ruler, the receiver of rents, and the collector of taxes. The rent,) Tj T*
(however, was not a competitive price, but consisted of the dues and) Tj T*
(services the forefathers had been accustomed to pay. This limited) Tj T*
(slavery, like all other slavery, was wasteful, as it did not give to the) Tj T*
(individual the strongest motive to increase the quantity and to improve) Tj T*
(the quality of his service. Trade became limited in almost every) Tj T*
(direction. Crafts and gilds arrogated to themselves the right of) Tj T*
(employment in their industries. No matter what talent the son of a) Tj T*
(peasant might show, he usually found it impossible and always found it) Tj T*
(difficult to follow the occupation of his choice. Privilege pervaded all) Tj T*
(the life of that time. In such conditions economic friction is great.) Tj T*
(Men are kept in trades below their ability, while others gain command of) Tj T*
(monopolistic and unearned returns.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Yet through all the Middle Ages ran the forces of competition. The) Tj T*
(inefficiency of customary services was a constant invitation to) Tj T*
(competitors. Men were striving to break over the barriers of custom and) Tj T*
(prejudice. The strife for freedom was the vital economic force even of) Tj T*
(the Middle Ages. The industrial history of that time is largely the) Tj T*
(story of the struggle of the forces of competition against the bounds of) Tj T*
(custom.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(§ II. ECONOMIC HARMONY THROUGH COMPETITION) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Effect of modern forces on custom]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _The industrial events following the discovery of America) Tj T*
(strengthened the forces making for economic freedom._ Discoveries in the) Tj T*
(Western hemisphere opened up a wide field for the adventure and) Tj T*
(enterprise of Europe. Commerce is the strongest enemy of custom, and new) Tj T*
(opportunities gave a rude shock to the conservatism both of the manor) Tj T*
(and of the village. With the rapid growth of industry and manufactures,) Tj T*
(old methods broke down. In an open market custom declines; it flourishes) Tj T*
(best in sheltered places. Further, the movement of thought in the) Tj T*
(Reformation and the spirit of the time, expressing the principle of) Tj T*
(personal liberty, allowing the individual to follow his own opinions and) Tj T*
(take the consequences, were favorable to competition. Despite these) Tj T*
(facts the restraints of the national governments on trade continued) Tj T*
