(Revolution was one of the bitter fruits of the English policy of trade) Tj T*
(restriction.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Adam Smith's influence]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: The philosophy of natural law]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _Adam Smith's work advocating greater economic freedom had a profound) Tj T*
(influence upon public thought._ "The Wealth of Nations," the first) Tj T*
(great work on political economy, was published in the year 1776. That) Tj T*
(was the "psychological moment," as public thought was so prepared for it) Tj T*
(that it had its maximum possible influence. The year of the American) Tj T*
(Declaration of Independence gave the most striking object lesson on the) Tj T*
(evils of a selfish colonial policy that interfered on a grand scale with) Tj T*
(economic freedom. The old customs had become ill fitted to life, ill) Tj T*
(adapted to the rapid industrial changes that were going on. What was) Tj T*
(needed in many directions, both in politics and in industry, was) Tj T*
(negative action by the government, the repeal of the old laws, the) Tj T*
(overthrow of old abuses. The French Revolution, following a few years) Tj T*
(later, emphasized this thought in the political field. The philosophers) Tj T*
(of the time believed in a "natural law" in industry and politics. The) Tj T*
(reformers of the time wished to throw off the trammels of the past and) Tj T*
(to give men opportunity to exert themselves "naturally." In America the) Tj T*
(old abuses never had taken deep root, as the conditions of a new) Tj T*
(continent were not favorable to monopoly and privilege. Although the) Tj T*
(movement for the repeal of medieval laws has continued in Europe from) Tj T*
(1776 till the present time, yet to-day custom is stronger in Europe than) Tj T*
(in America. Serfdom was not abolished until the nineteenth century in) Tj T*
(Austria and southeastern Europe, and not until a few years ago in) Tj T*
(Russia. Many economic and cultural forces furthered this movement, but) Tj T*
(the most powerful intellectual force in its favor was the work of Adam) Tj T*
(Smith. So strong an impression did Smith's book make, that in the minds) Tj T*
(of men "free trade" became almost identical in thought with political) Tj T*
(economy, whereas that was but the temporary economic problem of the) Tj T*
(eighteenth century.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: The doctrine of the economic harmonies]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. _The doctrine of the "economic harmonies" is the extremest form of) Tj T*
(belief in the virtues of competition._ Every truth in political) Tj T*
(philosophy finds some exaggerated expression. The main task of the) Tj T*
(student is to determine what shade of gray things are, rather than) Tj T*
(whether they are white or black. The belief in the benefits of) Tj T*
(competition and the virtues of economic freedom found expression in the) Tj T*
(doctrine of "the economic harmonies." This is the faith that if men are) Tj T*
(left entirely free to do as their interest dictates, the highest and) Tj T*
