(CHAPTER 45) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(USE, COINAGE, AND VALUE OF MONEY) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(§ I. THE PRECIOUS METALS AS MONEY) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Money defined and reviewed]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _Money we have defined as a material means of payment and medium of) Tj T*
(exchange, generally accepted and passing from hand to hand._ The origin) Tj T*
(and function of money were set forth in the study of capital. The) Tj T*
(subject must now be approached from a different side and with the) Tj T*
(two-fold purpose of seeing whether there is anything peculiar in the) Tj T*
(relation of money to the general problem of value, and what is the) Tj T*
(influence of the action of the state on the value of money. The) Tj T*
(definition of money implies several ideas. First, the words "generally) Tj T*
(accepted means of payment" imply that money, as something bearing the) Tj T*
(stamp of social approval, has a peculiar social character, is not an) Tj T*
(ordinary good. Second, the definition implies that money itself must be) Tj T*
(a thing having value, otherwise it could not serve as a medium of) Tj T*
(exchange. Exchange means the taking and giving of things of value. Money) Tj T*
(is, therefore, not merely an order for goods, as a card or paper) Tj T*
(requesting payment; it is itself a thing of value, though this value may) Tj T*
(be due solely to its possessing the money function. This point is one of) Tj T*
(the most difficult in the subject. Third, the definition implies that) Tj T*
(money is a material thing. The telegram when transferring an order for) Tj T*
(the payment of money, the spoken word, the promise to pay, etc., are not) Tj T*
(money. Fourth, it implies that money passes from hand to hand, is a) Tj T*
(thing that can be handled, and is or can be bodily transported.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Difficulty in applying the definition]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
