(The application of the definition is not always easy, for money shades) Tj T*
(off into other things that serve the same purpose and are related in) Tj T*
(nature. Even special students differ as to the border-line of the) Tj T*
(concept, but as to the general nature of money there is essential) Tj T*
(agreement. In many problems it appears to be at the same time like and) Tj T*
(unlike other things of value, and just wherein lies the difference often) Tj T*
(is difficult to determine. The use of money is of such social) Tj T*
(importance, and it touches so many practical interests, that it raises) Tj T*
(many questions of a political and ethical nature. There are perhaps more) Tj T*
(popular errors on this than on any other one subject in economics. Yet) Tj T*
(the general principles of money are as fully understood and as firmly) Tj T*
(established as any parts of economics.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Standard, or primary, money]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Gold-using countries]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _The precious metals, gold and silver, are the standard, or primary,) Tj T*
(moneys in the world to-day._ Primary, typical, standard money is the) Tj T*
(unit in which the value of the money of a country is expressed, no) Tj T*
(matter what its form is; the standard is a certain weight and fineness) Tj T*
(of a particular metal. Coins of this standard are called full, or real,) Tj T*
(money by some writers who deny the title of money to everything else. It) Tj T*
(has been shown before that there has been an evolution in the use of) Tj T*
(money. The more efficient forms, gold and silver, have competed with) Tj T*
(copper, iron, tin, cattle, salt, tobacco. In this contest silver had) Tj T*
(proved itself a few centuries ago to be the fittest medium of exchange,) Tj T*
(but in the last century gold has, among the leading nations, been) Tj T*
(displacing silver rapidly. In a higher degree than any other material,) Tj T*
(gold has the qualities of a good standard money in rich and industrially) Tj T*
(developed communities. The gold-using countries to-day are those of the) Tj T*
(western world. England for perhaps two centuries practically has had) Tj T*
(gold as its standard money; the United States since 1834 \(except for the) Tj T*
(period of paper money from 1862 to 1879\); France since about the year) Tj T*
(1855, at which time she shifted from silver under the working of the) Tj T*
(bimetallic law; and Germany, then more backward industrially, since) Tj T*
(1873. Australia and Japan have reached that result only within the last) Tj T*
(few years, and Italy, Russia, India, Mexico--even China and other) Tj T*
(Oriental countries--are striving to attain it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Subordinate kinds of money]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In all these countries other kinds of money are used side by side with) Tj T*
(gold and silver. The actual money consists of a wide and confusing) Tj T*
(variety: silver, nickel, copper, paper in various forms and issued by) Tj T*
(various authorities. But among all the kinds, either gold or silver is) Tj T*
(found standing preëminent and in a peculiar position. The difficulties) Tj T*
(of the money problem must be attacked at the point of standard money) Tj T*
(where it is nearest to ordinary value problems and is less complicated) Tj T*
(than when the various money substitutes are included. Most of the) Tj T*
(fallacies regarding money have arisen not about standard money, but) Tj T*
(about paper and light-weight silver.) Tj T*
