(§ II. THE QUANTITY THEORY OF MONEY) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: The money use]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _The fundamental use that money serves is to apportion incomes of) Tj T*
(goods so as to make them yield the maximum gratification._ Money first) Tj T*
(increases utility by increasing the ease with which exchange takes) Tj T*
(place. Like any tool or agent, it is valued for what it does or helps to) Tj T*
(do. But further, it enhances the sum of enjoyments by the division of) Tj T*
(goods into proper quantities, making them available at the best time. It) Tj T*
(follows from the principle of diminishing utility that the particular) Tj T*
(time at which goods are available for wants has an essential bearing on) Tj T*
(their value. A hundred loaves of bread in the hands of a single) Tj T*
(individual would mold long before they could be consumed. Money enables) Tj T*
(men in society to acquire these hundred loaves in a series so that they) Tj T*
(can be used when most needed. Money is the most successful device man) Tj T*
(has ever discovered for distributing the supplies of a journey along its) Tj T*
(course, and the goods of daily need over a period of time. The use of) Tj T*
(money as a storehouse of value is merely an extreme case of keeping) Tj T*
(things for the future when they will have a greater gratifying power.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
