(nature of the money demand. Money is sought, in order to form a cash) Tj T*
(reserve, up to a point where the loss from keeping it balances the) Tj T*
(probable gain. The money use is subject to the law of diminishing) Tj T*
(utility; beyond a certain point its added convenience is purchased at) Tj T*
(too great cost. Every man may be thought of as having an average, or) Tj T*
(usual, money demand, which is that proportion of his income that gives) Tj T*
(him more utility retained in money form than if at once expended. A man) Tj T*
(with an income and expenditure of fifty dollars a month paid monthly has) Tj T*
(use ordinarily for no more than fifty dollars as his cash reserve. While) Tj T*
(under ordinary circumstances this is his maximum demand, various) Tj T*
(circumstances may diminish it. If his expenses are distributed in two) Tj T*
(equal parts \(the one on pay-day, the other thirty days later\) his) Tj T*
(average money demand is twenty-five dollars, not fifty dollars. If most) Tj T*
