([Sidenote: The quantity theory of money]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _The quantity theory of money is that, other things being equal, the) Tj T*
(value of money falls as its quantity increases, and vice versa._ This is) Tj T*
(an abstract statement of a concrete and difficult problem. The phrase) Tj T*
("other things being equal" betokens the statement of a tendency where) Tj T*
(there are several unknown factors. In recent discussion the quantity) Tj T*
(theory of money has been questioned by some critics; yet it is held by) Tj T*
(most economists to be merely the general law of value as applied to) Tj T*
(money. There are three sets of facts to be brought into relationship) Tj T*
(with each other in the quantity theory: \(1\) amount of business or) Tj T*
(exchanges to be effected; \(2\) the methods by which this is done; \(3\) the) Tj T*
(amount of money available to do it. According to the quantity theory we) Tj T*
(must expect that when conditions \(1\) and \(2\) remain fixed, the value of) Tj T*
(money will vary inversely as its quantity. This conclusion follows from) Tj T*
(the conception of the money demand as the value of circulating medium) Tj T*
(that bears an average proportion to the value of goods exchanged.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
