(quantity theory hold in these conditions? Several objections to the) Tj T*
(quantity theory are presented. It is said, first, that prices do not) Tj T*
(vary exactly with the per capita circulation of different countries at a) Tj T*
(given moment. The per capita circulation in Mexico may be five dollars) Tj T*
(and in the United States twenty-five dollars, while prices are much less) Tj T*
(than five times as great here as in Mexico. Secondly, it is said that) Tj T*
(prices do not vary directly with changes in the amount of money in a) Tj T*
(given country. There is now perhaps five times as much money per capita) Tj T*
(in the United States as fifty years ago and yet prices are not five) Tj T*
(times as high. Thirdly, it is said that credit methods change, and) Tj T*
(therefore that money does not fix prices. Fourthly, it is said that even) Tj T*
(if true of primary money the theory fails to apply to actual conditions) Tj T*
(with many forms of money in circulation side by side. Fifthly, it is) Tj T*
(said that there are too many unknown quantities to permit the rule to be) Tj T*
(used.) Tj T*
