(Inductive demonstration of broadly stated economic principles is) Tj T*
(difficult, but in no other economic problem is laboratory experiment so) Tj T*
(nearly possible as in that of money. Many inflations and contractions of) Tj T*
(the circulating medium have occurred, now in a single country, again in) Tj T*
(the entire world, and the local or general results have served to) Tj T*
(exemplify richly the working of the quantity principle. With the scanty) Tj T*
(yield of silver- and gold-mines in the Middle Ages, prices were low.) Tj T*
(After the discovery of America, especially in the sixteenth century,) Tj T*
(quantities of silver flowed into Europe. The great rise of prices that) Tj T*
(occurred was explained by the keenest thinkers of that day along the) Tj T*
(essential lines of the quantity theory, though there were many monetary) Tj T*
(fallacies current at the time. The experience in England during the) Tj T*
(Napoleonic wars, when the money of England was inflated and prices rose) Tj T*
(above those of the Continent, led to the modern formulation of the) Tj T*
(theory by Ricardo and others. The discovery of gold in California and) Tj T*
(Australia, in 1848-50, increased the gold supply marvelously, and gold) Tj T*
(prices rose throughout the world. Between 1870 and 1890 the production) Tj T*
(of gold fell off greatly while its use as money increased and prices) Tj T*
(fell. A great increase of gold production has occurred in the period) Tj T*
(since 1890. In part the rising prices from 1897 to 1902 are explicable) Tj T*
(as the periodic upswing of confidence and credit, but in part doubtless) Tj T*
(they are due to the stimulus of increasing gold supplies. These are but) Tj T*
(a few of many instances in monetary history which, taken together, make) Tj T*
(an argument of probability in favor of the quantity theory so strong as) Tj T*
(to constitute practically its inductive proof.) Tj T*
