() Tj T*
(Gresham's law needs some explanation, for it is frequently) Tj T*
(misunderstood. "Bad" money means money that has not the bullion value) Tj T*
(equal to its money value, money that is either debased in quality or) Tj T*
(light in weight. But not every piece of bad money will drive out every) Tj T*
(piece of good money. If that were so, a single bad penny would drive out) Tj T*
(of circulation all the gold. The law applies only under certain) Tj T*
(conditions. The "good" will leave the country only if the total amount) Tj T*
(of money in circulation is in excess of what would be needed if all were) Tj T*
(of full weight or best quality. Paradoxically speaking, if there is not) Tj T*
(too much of the bad money, it is just as good as the good money. The) Tj T*
(good money may not leave the country. It may be hoarded, or be picked) Tj T*
(out by banks and savings-institutions to retain as their reserve, or it) Tj T*
(may be melted for use in the arts. Gresham's "law" is thus a practical) Tj T*
(precept: keep the amount of token or light-weight coin limited to the) Tj T*
(field of its peculiar use, or it will cause the other forms, the fuller) Tj T*
(weight money, to leave for a better market. That better market may be) Tj T*
(the melting-pot or it may be a foreign country.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
