() Tj T*
(1. _Government paper money may be defined as money for which a) Tj T*
(seigniorage of one hundred per cent. is charged._ The order in the study) Tj T*
(of the money question is from seigniorage to paper money, because paper) Tj T*
(money embodies the principle of seigniorage in its extremest form. The) Tj T*
(issue of paper money grew out of the practice of debasing metal. The) Tj T*
(gain of seigniorage from paper money is greater and is just as easily) Tj T*
(secured. Government paper money is sometimes called "political money,") Tj T*
(in contrast with money whose value rests on the value of its material.) Tj T*
(In this sense, however, all coins containing an element of seigniorage,) Tj T*
(or monopoly value, are to that degree "political" money. The typical) Tj T*
(paper money is irredeemable, that is, it cannot be turned into bullion) Tj T*
(money on demand. It was simply put into circulation with the) Tj T*
(legal-tender quality. The "legal-tender" quality is the declaration of) Tj T*
(the government that the paper money must be accepted by citizens as a) Tj T*
(legal discharge for debts due them. The object of this is to compel) Tj T*
(people to use it as money whether they will or not. The purpose of the) Tj T*
(government in thus employing its power over the circulating medium is) Tj T*
(usually to profit, that is, to secure the value of the seigniorage for) Tj T*
(public purposes. Paper money differs from bank-notes in that it does not) Tj T*
(depend for its redemption on the credit of the issuer. It differs from) Tj T*
