(2. _The most notable examples of paper money in the eighteenth century) Tj T*
(were the American colonial currencies, the continental notes, and the) Tj T*
(French assignats._ In all the American colonies before the Revolution) Tj T*
(notes or bills of credit were issued which were in most cases legal) Tj T*
(tender. Without exception they were issued in large amounts and without) Tj T*
(exception they depreciated. Parliament forbade the issues, but to no) Tj T*
(effect. The continental notes were issued by the Continental Congress in) Tj T*
(the first year of the war \(1775\), and for the next five years. The) Tj T*
(object at first was to anticipate taxes, and it was expected that the) Tj T*
(states would redeem and destroy the notes, but this was not done. The) Tj T*
(notes passed at par for a time, but depreciated rapidly as their number) Tj T*
(increased. The country had less than $10,000,000 of coin before the war,) Tj T*
(and when, in 1780, over $200,000,000 of notes were in circulation they) Tj T*
(were completely discredited; hence the phrase "not worth a continental.") Tj T*
(Specie quickly came back into use. A few years later the leaders of the) Tj T*
(French Revolution, failing to learn the lesson of the American) Tj T*
(experience, issued, on the security of land, notes called assignats in) Tj T*
(such enormous quantities that they became worth no more than the paper) Tj T*
(on which they were printed. In a figurative sense they may be said to) Tj T*
(have fallen to their "bullion" value.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: More recent examples of paper money]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
