(§ III. THEORIES OF POLITICAL MONEY) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Commodity-money theory]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _The commodity-money theorists declare that government is powerless) Tj T*
(to influence value, or to impart value to paper by law._ There are two) Tj T*
(extreme views regarding the nature of paper money, and a third which) Tj T*
(endeavors to find the truth between these two. First is that of the) Tj T*
(commodity-money theorists, or the cost-of-production theorists, who will) Tj T*
(not admit that there is any other basis for the value of money than the) Tj T*
(cost of the material that is in it. Money made of paper, on a printing) Tj T*
(press, has a cost almost negligibly small, and, therefore, they say it) Tj T*
(can have no value. The fact that it does circulate, and is treated as if) Tj T*
(it had value, is explained by the commodity theorists as follows: While) Tj T*
(the paper note is a mere promise to pay, with no value in itself, it is) Tj T*
(accepted because of the hope of its redemption, just as is any private) Tj T*
(note. Depreciation in this view is due to loss of confidence; the rise) Tj T*
(toward par measures the hope of repayment. Such a view overlooks the) Tj T*
(feature in which paper money differs from ordinary credit paper. The) Tj T*
(value of one's promise to pay depends on his reputation and his) Tj T*
(resources; the resources constitute the basis of value. Bonds have value) Tj T*
(because they yield interest and are payable at a definite time in) Tj T*
(standard money. But paper money, lacking this basis for its value, has) Tj T*
(another basis in its money use, in its power to buy goods. The money) Tj T*
(demand in connection with the monopoly power of government over the) Tj T*
(money supply, furnishes a satisfactory logical explanation of the value) Tj T*
(of paper money.) Tj T*
