([Sidenote: Fiat-money theory]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _The fiat-money advocates assert that government has unlimited power) Tj T*
(to maintain the value of paper money by conferring upon it the) Tj T*
(legal-tender quality._ The meaning of fiat is "let there be," and the) Tj T*
(fiat-money advocates believe that the government has but to say, "let it) Tj T*
(be money," to invest paper with value. The typical fiat advocates in) Tj T*
(the United States were the "Greenbackers," those voters who wished to) Tj T*
(retain the paper money issued in the Civil War, and to increase its) Tj T*
(amount greatly. They saw in paper money an unlimited source of income to) Tj T*
(the government. They proposed the payment of the national debt, the) Tj T*
(support of the government without taxes, and the loan of unlimited money) Tj T*
(without interest to citizens. All might live in luxury if the extreme) Tj T*
(fiat-money theorists could realize their dream. There are still some) Tj T*
(survivors of this faith in the power of the government fiat. The) Tj T*
(depreciation that has taken place in every case where government notes) Tj T*
(have been issued, they declare to be due to a too mild enforcement of) Tj T*
(the law of legal tender. To them the fact that paper money may circulate) Tj T*
(for a time at par appears a reason why it always should. They do not) Tj T*
(admit that there is a saturation point in the use of money, and that its) Tj T*
(use is still further limited by the fear of larger issues. They do not) Tj T*
(see that the ultimate basis of the value of paper money is economic,--is) Tj T*
(in its money use, not in the fiat of the government.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Theoretical possibility of a good paper money]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. _A sound theory of paper money makes it a special case of monopoly) Tj T*
(value._ It has been seen that the power of almost every monopoly over) Tj T*
(price is relative, not absolute. As the power of a great private) Tj T*
(corporation over the price of its product is limited, so is that of the) Tj T*
(government over the value of political money. The money use is the) Tj T*
(source of value to the paper notes. Business conditions remaining) Tj T*
(unchanged, the limit of possible issue without depreciation is the) Tj T*
(number of units in circulation before the paper money was issued, the) Tj T*
(saturation point of full-weight and full-value coins. Because) Tj T*
(governments generally have not stopped at that point, paper money has) Tj T*
(depreciated. Popular error and selfish interests force legislation) Tj T*
(beyond the reasonable limit. In a few cases only have there been public) Tj T*
(integrity and courage enough to retrace the steps before great harm) Tj T*
(resulted. It is principally this lack of control that prevents paper) Tj T*
(money from being a good circulating medium.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Influence of law on value]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is sometimes said that government cannot affect value in any way, but) Tj T*
(it can do so in many ways. Certainly one of the most remarkable is by) Tj T*
(the use of its monopoly power over the medium of exchange, whereby it) Tj T*
(can, under certain conditions, cause a piece of paper to have the value) Tj T*
(of a piece of gold. Thereby at the same time it affects the interests of) Tj T*
(nearly every member of society, raising or lowering the value of many) Tj T*
(kinds of property, and of many incomes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
