(to the state officials. State banks are those organized under special) Tj T*
(state banking laws. They are usually subject to inspection by state-bank) Tj T*
(commissioners, must make regular reports, and are required to comply) Tj T*
(with certain rules as to their reserves, rates, and investments. In any) Tj T*
(case they do not issue bank-notes, because the national laws now tax the) Tj T*
(notes of state banks so heavily that they are unprofitable. National) Tj T*
(banks, the largest and most important portion of our banking system,) Tj T*
(were authorized by law in 1863, during the Civil War. They are subject) Tj T*
(to stricter regulation and inspection than are other banks, and that) Tj T*
(regulation is perhaps an advantage to them, as it strengthens public) Tj T*
(confidence in their stability. Yet this regulation does not insure the) Tj T*
