(CHAPTER 49) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(TAXATION IN ITS RELATION TO VALUE) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(§ I. PURPOSES OF TAXATION) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Taxation defined]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _Provision for the expense of organized government is the fundamental) Tj T*
(purpose of taxation._ Taxation may be defined as the taking by the) Tj T*
(government of private property for public uses. This implies a certain) Tj T*
(degree of compulsion. When the national government accepts ten million) Tj T*
(dollars in trust for the Carnegie Institution, it is not taxation,) Tj T*
(though wealth is given for public uses. The effects of taxation pervade) Tj T*
(all industrial affairs, but they will be discussed here only in relation) Tj T*
(to the value of goods and to the distribution of incomes. By taxation) Tj T*
(the government interferes with the individual's free choice and with the) Tj T*
(impersonal economic forces. It expends income in different ways from) Tj T*
(those which would be chosen by the individual.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Taxation for public defense]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The primary purpose of taxation is public defense. War often has driven) Tj T*
(men into closer social relations. Public defense requires sacrifice on) Tj T*
(the part of the family and of the individual. In family or patriarchal) Tj T*
(communities all share a common income and combine in the common defense,) Tj T*
(but self-preservation compels such small communities to form a larger,) Tj T*
(stronger state for the common defense. Personal service in the field) Tj T*
(gives place to money taxes permitting a more regular, continuing, and) Tj T*
(perfect organization of military forces.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: To preserve domestic order]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Next comes the need of civil government to insure domestic tranquillity.) Tj T*
(As political unity grows, the citizens need less often protection) Tj T*
