() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Shifting and incidence of taxation]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. _After every change in taxation, competition among bargainers goes on) Tj T*
(and a new equilibrium of prices results._ The citizen who pays a tax) Tj T*
(into the public treasury is not always the one whose income is reduced) Tj T*
(in the long run. In most cases the final and regular burden of the tax) Tj T*
(is distributed over a number of incomes. The passing on of the burden is) Tj T*
(called the shifting of the tax; the location of the final burden is) Tj T*
(called the incidence of the tax. The lawmaker cannot tell exactly where) Tj T*
(the weight will fall. The principles of value give some guidance in the) Tj T*
(inquiry, but the workings of the principle are difficult to follow.) Tj T*
(Certain it is that the new tax, both in its collection and in its) Tj T*
(expenditure, becomes a new influence in industry. Some occupations are) Tj T*
(made more attractive, others less so. Some places are made more, others) Tj T*
(less, desirable to live in. As property thus fluctuates in value, as) Tj T*
(investments become more or less remunerative, the market price of) Tj T*
(corporation stocks rises and falls. The rate of adjustment varies) Tj T*
(greatly under different conditions. The inflow and the outflow of labor) Tj T*
(and capital are more or less rapid in the various industries.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Many personal incomes affected]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The fact that a change in taxation is a disturbing element in price is) Tj T*
(not to be thought insignificant merely because "all comes out right in) Tj T*
(the end." Every change in taxation is an element of uncertainty in) Tj T*
(business and increases the fortunes of some men at the expense of) Tj T*
(others. Hence no considerable change should be made without good reasons) Tj T*
(in its favor. The older taxes have the virtue of stability, but in many) Tj T*
(cases they have grown out of harmony with the industrial conditions.) Tj T*
(While, therefore, from time to time there is a real need of a reform in) Tj T*
(the tax system, it should not be undertaken without recognizing the many) Tj T*
(and complex interests involved.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(§ III. PRINCIPLES AND PRACTICE) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Various standards of justice suggested]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _Taxation should be adjusted with reference to the general social) Tj T*
(interest._ Many standards have been suggested to measure the) Tj T*
(distribution of the burden of taxation, such as benefit, equality, and) Tj T*
(ability. Each of these terms is capable of various interpretations which) Tj T*
(have changed from time to time. The benefit derived by any citizen from) Tj T*
(most of the public services evidently cannot be measured with exactness.) Tj T*
