(The standard of equality cannot be applied in any literal sense to) Tj T*
(strong and weak, to rich and poor. It is possible, however, to interpret) Tj T*
(equality with reference not to objective goods, but to the psychic) Tj T*
(sacrifice occasioned by taxation. Ability thus is of many kinds and may) Tj T*
(be differently understood. Some think ability to bear taxation is "in) Tj T*
(exact proportion to the money income"; others believe that it increases) Tj T*
(at a greater rate than money income, and favor, therefore, progressive) Tj T*
(taxation, that is, higher rates on the larger incomes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Social welfare as the aim]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The conflicting interests of the classes in each period are to some) Tj T*
(degree softened by the social conscience, and taxes are adjusted) Tj T*
(according to a vaguely held ideal of the social welfare. Social) Tj T*
(expediency, more or less broadly interpreted, determines who shall be) Tj T*
(taxed and what will give the best social results. The exemptions from) Tj T*
(taxation in feudal times were great, and viewed from our standpoint were) Tj T*
(inequitable, for it was the upper classes who escaped while the peasants) Tj T*
(bore all the burdens. The landlords and nobility who were assumed to be) Tj T*
(performing important social functions, often had outgrown their) Tj T*
(usefulness. Exemptions are granted liberally in most states to-day for) Tj T*
(some purposes and to some classes of citizens; to educational,) Tj T*
(religious, and charitable institutions; to the homes of priests and) Tj T*
(ministers; to homesteads purchased with pension money, etc. California) Tj T*
(alone of all the states in the Union continued until 1903 to tax) Tj T*
(churches and private schools. The social interest requires that taxes be) Tj T*
(both elastic and productive, so that the needs of the government shall) Tj T*
(be amply provided for. The harmonizing of these needs in the laws of) Tj T*
(taxation requires a high degree of wisdom, of foresight, and of) Tj T*
(integrity, in the legislator and in the citizen. No hard-and-fast rule) Tj T*
(for the apportioning of taxes can be laid down. The decision must be) Tj T*
(made in each generation by social opinion, guided by the social) Tj T*
(conscience.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Principles of administration]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _The administration of taxation should be economical, certain, and) Tj T*
(uniform._ Whatever taxes are adopted, whether on property or income,) Tj T*
(whether at a proportional or a progressive rate, their justice and) Tj T*
(expediency depend largely on their administration. Principle and) Tj T*
(practice in this as in most affairs may go far apart. Some laws are more) Tj T*
(easily and economically executed than others. The time of collection) Tj T*
(should be as convenient as possible for the citizen, and the mode of) Tj T*
(payment should be the most simple. As to the time, method of payment,) Tj T*
(and amount, the utmost certainty is desirable. Taxation that is) Tj T*
(variable, shifting, dependent on personal whim and favoritism, is) Tj T*
(despotism. Above all, the administration of the law should be uniform) Tj T*
(and impartial,--yet this is a principle most frequently departed from in) Tj T*
(practice. The assessment of taxes has to be intrusted to men with) Tj T*
(fallible judgment, imperfect knowledge, and selfish interests. The) Tj T*
(assessor is as near a despot as any agent of popular government to-day.) Tj T*
