(affected._ The general attitude of economic students for a century and a) Tj T*
(half has been favorable to a large measure of freedom in foreign trade.) Tj T*
(But the actual practice of nations is opposed to the principles laid) Tj T*
(down by the philosophers and accepted by nearly all serious students of) Tj T*
(the question. Germany adopted very restrictive measures under Bismarck) Tj T*
(in 1879 and by a recent law has discouraged trade still further. France,) Tj T*
(Italy, and other smaller nations of Europe have strong protective) Tj T*
(tariffs. The United States has followed a restrictive policy for the) Tj T*
(last century almost unvaryingly. The explanation of this contradiction) Tj T*
(is not entirely simple. Free trade is not the most desirable thing for) Tj T*
(every one. Great interests are affected by foreign trade and certain of) Tj T*
(these interests are able to dominate legislation. The general) Tj T*
(proposition of free trade between nations, as advocated by most) Tj T*
(economists since Adam Smith, is rejected by a majority of the people, by) Tj T*
(the politicians, and by the legislators.) Tj T*
