(§ I. THE NATURE AND CLAIMS OF PROTECTION) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Nature of a tariff for revenue]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. _A protective tariff is a schedule of import duties so arranged as to) Tj T*
(give appreciably more favorable conditions to some domestic industries) Tj T*
(than they would enjoy with free trade._ Tariff duties were first laid to) Tj T*
(get revenues for the government. The first effect of the tariff is the) Tj T*
(same as that of any tax that enters as a new factor into enterpriser's) Tj T*
(cost--the domestic price of the taxed article tends to rise. Other) Tj T*
(results then follow. If the article cannot be produced within the) Tj T*
(country \(as oranges, spices, and coffee, in England, Norway, and) Tj T*
(Sweden\), its consumption is reduced. The lessening of demand may affect) Tj T*
(somewhat the price in the producing country and may compel the foreign) Tj T*
(producers to sell each unit for less than before. As such a tariff does) Tj T*
(not increase home production, it is for revenue, not for protection.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Effects upon home industry]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But if the article can be produced in the importing country at the new) Tj T*
(price, "home industries" will start. If the whole demand at home is thus) Tj T*
(supplied, imports stop and therewith stop all revenues to the government) Tj T*
(from that source. This is a prohibitive or completely protective tariff.) Tj T*
(Most tariffs combine the characters both of revenue and protective) Tj T*
(measures. Where the freight charges are low along the coast and on the) Tj T*
(main lines of transportation, some imports take place; while farther) Tj T*
(inland, where freight charges are high, some home production of the same) Tj T*
(goods takes place. A tariff that reduces imports but does not cut them) Tj T*
(off entirely is either a revenue tariff with incidental protection or a) Tj T*
(protective tariff with incidental revenue. The difference is partly one) Tj T*
(of legislative intention, partly one of degree only.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: The beginning of the tariff under the Constitution]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. _The tariff question has been the most discussed of economic) Tj T*
(questions in American politics._ The tariff bill passed by the first) Tj T*
(session of Congress in 1789 was primarily a revenue measure with rates) Tj T*
(averaging only about five per cent.; but incidentally it was protective) Tj T*
(\(as most tariffs are\), being laid on imports of iron and cloth, the) Tj T*
(production of which had been undertaken to some extent before, but which) Tj T*
(thus were further encouraged. Between 1808 and 1812, the United States) Tj T*
(and England were in constant disagreement, and our government repeatedly) Tj T*
(laid an embargo on British commerce, closing our ports to British ships,) Tj T*
(and British ports to our ships. The war from 1812 to 1815 almost) Tj T*
(annihilated American trade on the ocean. Added to this discouragement of) Tj T*
(foreign trade was the high tariff imposed, in the vain effort to get) Tj T*
(revenue from greatly decreased imports. Altogether these causes almost) Tj T*
(completely stopped importation and forced the American people to rely on) Tj T*
(their own efforts for such goods. Some industries having been) Tj T*
("stimulated" in a high degree, their destruction was threatened by the) Tj T*
(repeal of the high war tariffs. Many investments and interests were at) Tj T*
