([Sidenote: The tariff controversy before 1865]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The first period of real discussion of the protective policy was between) Tj T*
(1816 and 1846. The result of the first twelve years was an increase of) Tj T*
(the tariff rates which, in 1828, reached a high point. By the compromise) Tj T*
(of 1832, the rates were reduced by steps till 1841. Again from 1842 to) Tj T*
(1846 was a brief period of higher duties, followed by a policy which,) Tj T*
(relatively speaking, was one for revenue, from 1846 to 1860. Again in) Tj T*
(the Civil War, 1861-65, the rates were steadily increased without much) Tj T*
(discussion, the tariff not being the leading question at a time when) Tj T*
(the prosecution of the war was absorbing nearly all attention.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Recent discussion of the tariff]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The latest period of discussion was from 1874 to 1892. In the Tilden and) Tj T*
(Hayes campaign of 1876 the tariff was made the leading issue and the) Tj T*
(advocates of a lower tariff were very nearly successful. In 1880,) Tj T*
(protection again triumphed in the election of Garfield. In the election) Tj T*
(of Cleveland in 1884, the issue of tariff reform had some part, but no) Tj T*
(effective legislation on the subject was enacted in the next four years.) Tj T*
(In 1888, Cleveland was defeated in a campaign fought mainly on the) Tj T*
(tariff issue, and Harrison was elected as a pronounced protectionist. In) Tj T*
(1892, Cleveland was reëlected on the issue of tariff reform. From that) Tj T*
(time, however, there has been a lull in the discussion of the tariff) Tj T*
(question. The campaign of 1892 was the last presidential election in) Tj T*
(which the tariff was the dominant issue. Since 1896, the money question) Tj T*
(and imperialism have quite crowded the tariff issue off the stage.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: The "balance of trade" argument]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. _A leading argument in favor of a protective tariff is that by) Tj T*
(encouraging an excess of exports it maintains a favorable balance of) Tj T*
(trade._ This notion of the favorable balance of trade appears in several) Tj T*
(forms. One of these, already discussed in connection with foreign) Tj T*
(exchanges, is that the exports of a country in the form of merchandise) Tj T*
(must exceed the imports if the country is to prosper. The ideal) Tj T*
(cherished is to keep more merchandise constantly flowing out of the) Tj T*
(country than comes in. An interesting commentary on this delusion is the) Tj T*
(fact that this is the usual situation in poor debtor countries having) Tj T*
(constant interest payments to meet; while the opposite of the ideal is) Tj T*
(the situation in rich creditor countries. England for many years in the) Tj T*
(period of her greatest prosperity has had a constant excess of imports,) Tj T*
(these being goods to the value of the interest payments due to) Tj T*
(Englishmen from investments abroad.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: "To keep money at home"]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4. _Another argument is that the protective tariff keeps money at home) Tj T*
(which, if trade is free, will be sent abroad to buy foreign goods, thus) Tj T*
(impoverishing the country._ This is the "favorable balance of trade") Tj T*
