(advantage of canals is cheapness of operation due to the simplicity of) Tj T*
(the machinery needed and to the great loads that can be moved with) Tj T*
(small power. A cent a ton-mile is a paying rate on a canal. For heavy,) Tj T*
(slow-moving freight, the railroads can hardly rival the canals at their) Tj T*
(best. As canals, however, can be built only along a level country and) Tj T*
(where the water supply is at a high level, their construction is limited) Tj T*
(to a small portion of the country. The law of extensive diminishing) Tj T*
(returns applies strongly to the construction of canals. The first canals) Tj T*
(are easily constructed and economically operated, but it is only with) Tj T*
(greater cost and difficulty that the system can be successively) Tj T*
(extended. In temperate climates their use is limited by ice to a part of) Tj T*
(the year, and the summer's drought sometimes limits it still further. At) Tj T*
(its best, therefore, the small land-locked canal is fitted only to be a) Tj T*
(supplementary agent in the system of transportation wherever industry) Tj T*
(demands high speed and great regularity. Far different is the case of) Tj T*
(the oceanic canal in a tropical climate.) Tj T*
