(fertile soil.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: The practical limits of legislative reform]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The social institutions of any community are its answer, expressed in) Tj T*
(human consciousness and in formal laws, to this difficult problem of) Tj T*
(living together. Laws and ways for regulating industry may be good or) Tj T*
(bad. The good laws are those in harmony with human nature, giving the) Tj T*
(best motives for work and the greatest happiness both in the effort and) Tj T*
(in its reward. The merit of laws, therefore, is relative to human) Tj T*
(nature; those good for one kind of citizens may be bad for another. Men) Tj T*
(cannot be legislated into honesty without limit. The best that is) Tj T*
(possible is to enact laws that encourage the best in men as they are. A) Tj T*
(dishonest community neither has, nor is capable of choosing, men honest) Tj T*
(enough to supervise the others. Society cannot, by any amount of tugging) Tj T*
(and pulling on legislative boot-straps, lift itself above its own moral) Tj T*
(plane. But though the change in formal law cannot far precede, it may) Tj T*
(lag behind and retard, social progress. Law tardily adjusted to social) Tj T*
(needs tempts and corrupts men. A time has never been when a higher) Tj T*
(wisdom could not have corrected some ancient grievance, have leveled) Tj T*
(some unmerited inequality, and, by making laws as good as men were) Tj T*
(capable of administering at the moment, have freed their energies for) Tj T*
(further advances. It is only a spirit of moderate expectation that will) Tj T*
(not be cast down by the results of legal "reforms." Hence it cannot be) Tj T*
(hoped that abuses will not appear in the attempts to regulate private) Tj T*
(industry. Fallible men make mistakes and commit injustice, sometimes) Tj T*
(greater than that which they are seeking to prevent.) Tj T*
